#. §18: `h : A`
#. §19: `h : B`
#. §20: `\\<`
#. §21: `\\>`
#. §22: `⟨_, _⟩`
#. §23: $(G, 0, +)$
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:397
msgid "The §0 tactic breaks down an assumption into its individual parts.\n"
"\n"
"| before       | tactic                 | after                                   |\n"
"|:------------------ |:--------------------- - |:------------------------------------------ |\n"
"| §1        | §2 | §3 and §4                      |\n"
"| §5        | §6 | §7 and §8              |\n"
"| §9   | §10      | §11                                    |\n"
"| §12 | §13  | §14 and §15                     |\n"
"| §16        | §17   | a target with §18, a target with §19 |\n"
"\n"
"Type the brackets in the first four examples as §20 or §21.\n"
"Here, §22 is the *anonymous constructor*.\n"
"You can think of it as similar to the tuple notation in\n"
"“an abelian group is a tuple §23 such that ...”."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `refine ⟨..⟩`
#. §1: `A ≃ B`
#. §2: `⟨..⟩`
#. §3: `?_`
#. §4: `A ≃ B`
#. §5: `toFun : A → B`
#. §6: `invFun : B → A`
#. §7: `left_inv`
#. §8: `right_inv`
#. §9: `f : A → B`
#. §10: `g : B → A`
#. §11: `⊢ A ≃ B`
#. §12: ```
#. refine ⟨f, g, ?_, ?_⟩
#. ```
#. §13: `⊢ LeftInverse g f`
#. §14: `⊢ RightInverse g f`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:432
msgid "Tactic §0 splits a proof goal that asks for a *structure* — for example an\n"
"equivalence §1 — into one proof goal per field that you do not fill in yourself.\n"
"\n"
"Inside the anonymous constructor §2 you list the fields of the structure in order.\n"
"Each field you write as §3 becomes a new proof goal, the fields you supply directly\n"
"do not.\n"
"\n"
"## Example\n"
"\n"
"An equivalence §4 consists of a map §5, a backwards map §6,\n"
"and two proofs §7 and §8 that these undo each other.\n"
"So for given maps §9 and §10, the proof goal §11 is turned by\n"
"§12\n"
"into the two proof goals §13 and §14."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `revert h`
#. §1: `h`
#. §2: `h : A`
#. §3: `⊢ B`
#. §4: `⊢ A → B`
#. §5: `intro h`
#. §6: `revert h`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:452
msgid "The tactic §0 adds the assumption §1 as an implication premise to the proof goal:\n"
"from §2 and §3, we get §4.\n"
"\n"
"## Friends and relatives\n"
"\n"
"The tactic §5 does the exact opposite of §6."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rfl`
#. §1: `X = X`
#. §2: `rfl`
#. §3: `A = B`
#. §4: `A`
#. §5: `B`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:463
msgid "The tactic §0 proves §1.  More precisely, §2 closes every proof target of the form §3,\n"
"where §4 and §5 are identical in definition."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A ∨ B`
#. §1: `right`
#. §2: `left`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:470
msgid "If the proof target is of the form §0, you choose §1 to show the right side.\n"
"\n"
"## Friends and relatives\n"
"\n"
"With §2, you choose the left side accordingly."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `ring`
#. §1: `+, -, *, ^`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:479
msgid "The §0 tactic proves equations with the operations §1 in semirings,\n"
"in particular in ℕ, ℤ, ℚ, ℝ, …   It works particularly well in commutative rings."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h : X = Y`
#. §1: `h : X ↔ Y`
#. §2: `rw [h]`
#. §3: `X`
#. §4: `Y`
#. §5: `rw [←h]`
#. §6: `h`
#. §7: `Y`
#. §8: `X`
#. §9: `rw [h, g, ←f]`
#. §10: `h`
#. §11: `g`
#. §12: `f`
#. §13: `rw [h] at h₂`
#. §14: `h₂`
#. §15: `nth_rw`
#. §16: `h`
#. §17: `n`
#. §18: ```
#.    h : ∀ n, 2*n = f n
#.    ```
#. §19: ```
#.    h (n : ℕ) : 2*n = f n
#.    ```
#. §20: `rw [h]`
#. §21: `nth_rw k [h]`
#. §22: `k`
#. §23: `2*a + 2*b > 2*a`
#. §24: `rw [h]`
#. §25: `f a + 2*b > f a`
#. §26: `nth_rw 2 [h]`
#. §27: `2*a + f b > 2*a`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:487
msgid "If you have given an equation §0 or an equivalence §1 as an assumption or lemma,\n"
"you can use §2 to replace all occurrences of §3 in the proof target with §4.\n"
"\n"
"## Variants\n"
"\n"
"* §5 applies §6 backwards, i.e., replaces all §7 with §8.\n"
"* §9 applies §10, §11, and (backwards) §12.\n"
"* §13 performs the replacements in the assumption §14, not in the proof target\n"
"* §15: If §16 has arguments, e.g., §17 in\n"
"§18\n"
"   or in\n"
"§19\n"
"   §20 searches the proof target from left to right for a matching expression,\n"
"   and then replaces *all* occurrences of *the first* expression that the tactic finds.\n"
"   With §21, you can replace all occurrences of the §22th expression instead.\n"
"\n"
"  | before    | tactic       | after        |\n"
"  |:----------------- |:------------- - |:----------------- |\n"
"  | §23 | §24       | §25 |\n"
"  |                   | §26 | §27 |"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `simp`
#. §1: `mathlib`
#. §2: `@[simp]`
#. §3: `simp [h]`
#. §4: `h`
#. §5: `h`
#. §6: `simp [F]`
#. §7: `F`
#. §8: `simp only [h,f,g]`
#. §9: `h`
#. §10: `f`
#. §11: `g`
#. §12: `simp?`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:515
msgid "The §0 tactic attempts to apply a large number of lemmas to simplify a given expression.\n"
"(Technically, these are all lemmas in §1 that are marked with §2.)\n"
"\n"
"## Variants\n"
"\n"
"* §3 additionally uses the assumption §4 or the lemma §5 for simplification\n"
"* §6 additionally uses the definition of §7\n"
"* §8 exclusively uses the assumptions/lemmas/definitions §9, §10, and §11\n"
"* §12 shows you which lemmas were used"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `simp_rw`
#. §1: `rw`
#. §2: `simp_rw [h₁, h₂, h₃]`
#. §3: `h₁`
#. §4: `h₂`
#. §5: `h₃`
#. §6: `simp only []`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:528
msgid "§0 is a stronger form of §1.\n"
"§2 attempts to rewrite with each of §3, §4, §5, in this order.\n"
"The main differences to a plain rw are:\n"
"\n"
"* simp_rw applies each lemma/hypothesis as often as possible.\n"
"* simp_rw is better at rewriting under quantifiers and binders.\n"
"* simp_rw follows each step with §6"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `specialize h a₁ a₂`
#. §1: `have h := h a₁ a₂`
#. §2: `h : ∀ m₁ m₂, P m₁ m₂`
#. §3: `h : P a₁ a₂`
#. §4: `specialize`
#. §5: `have`
#. §6: `specialize h …`
#. §7: `h`
#. §8: `h`
#. §9: ```
#. have ha := h a₁ a₂
#. have hb := h b₁ b₂
#. ```
#. §10: ```
#. h : ∀ m₁ m₂, P m₁ m₂
#. ha : P a₁ a₂
#. hb : P b₁ b₂
#. ```
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:539
msgid "§0 is equivalent to §1: the tactic replaces an assumption\n"
"§2 with the special case §3.\n"
"\n"
"If you want to specialize multiple times, instead of §4 you should use §5,\n"
"since §6 overwrites the old assumption §7.\n"
"From the above assumption §8, you can obtain the following three assumptions with\n"
"§9\n"
":\n"
"§10"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `suffices h : P`
#. §1: `P`
#. §2: `P`
#. §3: `have h : P`
#. §4: `P`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:560
msgid "With §0, you introduce a proof section in which you show\n"
"that the desired proof goal follows from §1.\n"
"Then you prove §2.\n"
"\n"
"## Friends and relatives\n"
"§3 works the same way, except that you must first prove §4 before you can\n"
"continue with the main proof."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `symm`
#. §1: `=`
#. §2: `↔`
#. §3: `symm at h`
#. §4: `h`
#. §5: `h.symm`
#. §6: `symm at h`
#. §7: `h`
#. §8: `rw [←h]`
#. §9: ```
#.   symm at h
#.   rw [h]
#.   ```
#. §10: `rw [h.symm]`
#: Game/Doc/Tactic.lean:572
msgid "With §0 (for “symmetry”), you swap the sides of an equation (§1) or equivalence (§2) in the proof target.\n"
"\n"
"## Variants\n"
"* §3 operates on the assumption §4 instead of the proof target\n"
"* §5 is the result of §6 and can be used like §7\n"
"\n"
"Each of the following three tactics or tactic sequences therefore has the same effect:\n"
"* §8\n"
"* §9\n"
"* §10"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `trans`
